## 2. Operator identity, gates, signs and normalization

The author uses the same complete common physical P space, the same gated
\[
D=\sum_{a\to b}(1-n_b)E_{ab}(E_{ab}-q_a),
\]
the same overlapping-star magnetic Hamiltonian and the same original resolved or stipulated coherent-per-edge jumps as PRE. An integer divergence-free loop shift preserves Gauss and the full rotor space. Since the bounded magnetic and birth terms are matter matrices and commuting unit rotor shifts, they commute with the loop unitary and both quadratures on the complete matter space.

Thus the author's equation (1) is correct on the finite-electric core and has the stated bounded extension:
\[
F_v=[W_v^*,[h,W_v]]
=W_v^*hW_v+W_vhW_v^*-2h
=2K\sum_e(1-n_{b(e)})v_e^2.
\]
The linear charge term cancels in the symmetric electric difference. The gates remain, even on charged postbirth states. Positivity comes from the final diagonal expression, not from a claim that arbitrary double commutators are positive. Flux on occupied links need not be controlled by gated energy.

For \(C_v=(W_v+W_v^*)/2\) and \(S_v=(W_v-W_v^*)/(2i)\), expansion gives
\[
[C_v,[h,C_v]]+[S_v,[h,S_v]]=F_v.
\]
The dissipator annihilates both quadratures because they commute with every jump and its adjoint. It generally does not annihilate \(F_v\), which depends on matter vacancies. Nothing in this calculation removes later matter dynamics or proves that the finite-lag response is free.

The apparent sign difference from PRE is entirely the specified impulse convention. The author applies \(+\eta V\), so the state is \(e^{-i\eta V}\rho e^{i\eta V}\), and
\[
R_V(s;t)=i\operatorname{Tr}\rho_t[V,T_s^*V],\qquad
\partial_sR_V(0;t)=-\operatorname{Tr}\rho_t[V,[h,V]].
